Enable :has() selector parsing in stylo's Servo selector parser.

This targets the STYLO repository (https://github.com/servo/stylo), not
servo — it sits outside the numbered servo series. Stylo ships the full
:has() matching and relative-selector invalidation machinery (Gecko uses
it in production); the Servo-mode parser just declines to parse the
selector. Flipping the switch was validated end-to-end in the Copse
prototype on Linux against stylo rev 2d289c14: CSS.supports("selector(
:has(a))") reports true, a live `#box:has(span.present)` rule matches,
and dynamic invalidation works — toggling the matching child in and out
of the DOM restyles the parent both directions with no relayout glue on
the servo side. Upstream this belongs either as-is (if Servo is ready to
ship :has()) or behind a Servo pref threaded through ParserContext.

To try it: check out stylo at the rev pinned in servo's Cargo.lock,
apply, and add a [patch."https://github.com/servo/stylo"] section
redirecting stylo, selectors, servo_arc, stylo_atoms, stylo_derive,
stylo_dom, stylo_malloc_size_of, stylo_static_prefs, stylo_traits,
to_shmem and to_shmem_derive at the local checkout.

--- a/style/servo/selector_parser.rs
+++ b/style/servo/selector_parser.rs
@@ -594,7 +594,7 @@
 
     #[inline]
     fn parse_has(&self) -> bool {
-        false
+        true
     }
 
     #[inline]
